our own rubber, our own latex.

243
00:15:23,501 --> 00:15:27,500
That plantation is still in use today.

244
00:15:27,501 --> 00:15:34,300
At the height of its productivity, it had over 10,000 employees.

245
00:15:34,301 --> 00:15:37,800
As it sits right now, we have over 8,000 employees that

246
00:15:37,801 --> 00:15:42,880
harvest the natural latex rubber that's used in our tires today.

247
00:15:42,881 --> 00:15:45,880
While plantations were located all along the equator,

248
00:15:45,881 --> 00:15:48,880
rubber production was dominated by those in Southeast Asia.

249
00:15:48,881 --> 00:15:52,320
But all were about to become pawns in a global game of

250
00:15:52,321 --> 00:15:56,320
military strategy. The sudden entry into World

251
00:15:56,321 --> 00:15:59,320
War II forced the U.S. government to set goals for

252
00:15:59,321 --> 00:16:02,220
rubber consumption that far exceeded natural rubber

253
00:16:02,221 --> 00:16:05,640
production. Rubber was wrapped around every

254
00:16:05,641 --> 00:16:10,440
mile of wiring in every military and every domestic installation

255
00:16:10,441 --> 00:16:13,640
in this country. Every Sherman tank had half a

256
00:16:13,641 --> 00:16:16,180
ton of rubber. Every battleship that was to be

257
00:16:16,181 --> 00:16:20,820
at Pearl Harbor had over 20,000 rubber parts.

258
00:16:20,821 --> 00:16:23,620
After Pearl Harbor, the Japanese seized control of

259
00:16:23,621 --> 00:16:27,540
the massive rubber plantations in Southeast Asia, crippling the

260
00:16:27,541 --> 00:16:30,020
Allies' access to rubber supplies.

261
00:16:30,021 --> 00:16:32,740
It was an incredible crisis.

262
00:16:32,741 --> 00:16:37,220
At that moment, 95% of the world's rubber supply grew

263
00:16:37,221 --> 00:16:40,740
within 15 degrees longitude and latitude of Singapore, the

264
00:16:40,741 --> 00:16:43,140
first place the Japanese planned to hit.

265
00:16:43,141 --> 00:16:46,580
the Japanese took the entire world's supply, leaving us

266
00:16:46,581 --> 00:16:49,740
without a vital commodity of the industrial age.

267
00:16:49,741 --> 00:16:53,980
Roosevelt set into motion three draconian initiatives that in

268
00:16:53,981 --> 00:16:58,820
wartime situation were sort of, became really the great symbols

269
00:16:58,821 --> 00:17:00,900
of the spirit of American industry.

270
00:17:00,901 --> 00:17:03,300
The first thing that we did is set in motion the biggest

271
00:17:03,301 --> 00:17:06,020
recycling campaign in the history of the Earth.

272
00:17:06,021 --> 00:17:08,260
Americans were asked to salvage and collect

273
00:17:08,261 --> 00:17:12,260
used rubber, and a long list of other materials which could be

274
00:17:12,261 --> 00:17:16,260
repurposed for the war effort. These scrap drives were the

275
00:17:16,261 --> 00:17:19,260
subject of great publicity in the early days of the war, as a

276
00:17:19,261 --> 00:17:23,260
way for average citizens of all ages to support the country in

277
00:17:23,261 --> 00:17:27,260
its time of need. The second initiative required

278
00:17:27,261 --> 00:17:32,260
aid from citizens with expertise in natural resources.

279
00:17:32,261 --> 00:17:36,260
We sent plant explorers to every corner of the free world

280
00:17:36,261 --> 00:17:40,260
to squeeze every drop of latex we possibly could.

281
00:17:40,261 --> 00:17:44,260
above everything, we sent plant explorers back into the heart

282
00:17:44,261 --> 00:17:48,260
of the Amazon, not only to secure new sources of latex,

283
00:17:48,261 --> 00:17:53,260
but more importantly, to find a way to forever free ourselves

284
00:17:53,261 --> 00:17:57,260
of dependency on distant plantations.

285
00:17:57,261 --> 00:18:00,260
But it was the third directive that made the most vital

286
00:18:00,261 --> 00:18:05,260
impact, a decree to manufacture artificial rubber.

287
00:18:05,261 --> 00:18:08,260
The order went out to the synthetic chemists that they

288
00:18:08,261 --> 00:18:10,960
that they had no choice but to create an industry.

289
00:18:10,961 --> 00:18:15,260
They were told that if by 1943 you can't find a way

290
00:18:15,261 --> 00:18:17,860
to make a million metric tons of a product

291
00:18:17,861 --> 00:18:20,500
that has not even entered the developmental stages,

292
00:18:20,501 --> 00:18:22,400
the war will be lost.

293
00:18:22,401 --> 00:18:27,220
So a real push was made to develop our own synthetic rubber.

294
00:18:27,221 --> 00:18:31,220
The government actually nationalized many of the plants

295
00:18:31,221 --> 00:18:36,620
and took over and set priorities for the development work.

296
00:18:36,621 --> 00:18:39,320
And we were successful in doing that.

297
00:18:39,321 --> 00:18:44,360
The basic discoveries in the laboratory gave a good impetus to it,

298
00:18:44,460 --> 00:18:49,680
but it was the scale-up to production quantities needed for the war effort

299
00:18:49,681 --> 00:18:55,440
that really became almost a miracle of the same order as the Manhattan Project,

300
00:18:55,980 --> 00:19:00,400
that is a government industry, university research combine

301
00:19:00,401 --> 00:19:05,700
that really pushed as hard as it could to get somewhere in a very short period of time

302
00:19:05,701 --> 00:19:08,860
in the space of two years, producing hundreds of thousands

303
00:19:08,861 --> 00:19:13,200
of tons of a quite usable synthetic rubber.

304
00:19:13,201 --> 00:19:14,960
Quite important.

305
00:19:14,961 --> 00:19:18,420
Synthetic rubber, the chemical manipulation

306
00:19:18,421 --> 00:19:21,180
of elements that produced a substance which emulated the

307
00:19:21,181 --> 00:19:24,620
properties of natural rubber.

308
00:19:24,621 --> 00:19:27,220
Experiments with synthetics predated the war by two

309
00:19:27,221 --> 00:19:28,720
decades.

310
00:19:28,721 --> 00:19:31,620
German and American scientists grappled with petroleum-based

311
00:19:31,621 --> 00:19:35,580
formulas to create Buna N and neoprene.

312
00:19:35,581 --> 00:19:38,240
These early substitutes for rubber were precursors to the

313
00:19:38,241 --> 00:19:40,820
synthetic compounds that were directly responsible for meeting

314
00:19:40,821 --> 00:19:44,740
the military's demands. Those compounds provided the war

315
00:19:44,741 --> 00:19:47,380
machine the rubber resources needed to operate

316
00:19:47,381 --> 00:19:51,640
successfully. The complex process of creating

317
00:19:51,641 --> 00:19:54,780
synthetic rubber that was developed during World War II

318
00:19:54,781 --> 00:19:57,780
applied principles that are used in today's synthetic

319
00:19:57,781 --> 00:20:00,980
materials. Creating synthetic rubber is an

320
00:20:00,981 --> 00:20:03,980
art form rooted in elementary chemistry.

321
00:20:03,981 --> 00:20:06,180
Atoms group together to form molecules,

322
00:20:06,181 --> 00:20:08,620
and molecules are linked together to assemble strands

323
00:20:08,621 --> 00:20:11,780
of complex molecules, known as polymers.

324
00:20:11,781 --> 00:20:15,020
As more polymers are created, the massive strands entangle,

325
00:20:15,021 --> 00:20:16,620
much like spaghetti.

326
00:20:16,621 --> 00:20:20,700
Through vulcanization, you can essentially connect all

327
00:20:20,701 --> 00:20:24,700
those individual molecules, practically making one giant

328
00:20:24,701 --> 00:20:26,700
molecule of rubber.

329
00:20:26,701 --> 00:20:30,540
And if you could think, if you let a plate of spaghetti sit,

330
00:20:30,541 --> 00:20:33,340
pretty much everywhere the spaghetti touches another strand

331
00:20:33,341 --> 00:20:35,340
spaghetti, it's going to stick together.

332
00:20:35,341 --> 00:20:38,340
Chemists blend carefully measured ingredients using

333
00:20:38,341 --> 00:20:42,340
precise recipes to create the exact consistency desired.

334
00:20:42,341 --> 00:20:47,340
We've got an example of a very simplistic formula for you just

335
00:20:47,341 --> 00:20:51,340
to demonstrate the different building blocks that go into a

336
00:20:51,341 --> 00:20:55,340
rubber compound. This is the raw synthetic polymer.

337
00:20:55,341 --> 00:21:01,340
This is actually EPDM. It's an ethylene propylene with a

338
00:21:01,341 --> 00:21:07,340
with a diene monomer. With this we're going to incorporate some plasticizer.

339
00:21:07,341 --> 00:21:11,440
It's basically oil. It's a plasticizer that has a specific

